DEEP[1]=("Chapter 5 is named Karma-Sannyāsa Yoga, the yoga of renouncing action. It is one of the shorter chapters, 29 verses, and resolves the tension between action and renunciation raised in chapters 3 and 4.",
 "<i>Sannyāsaṃ karmaṇāṃ kṛṣṇa</i>, “renunciation of actions, Kṛṣṇa”. <i>Punar yogaṃ ca śaṃsasi</i>, “and again you praise yoga”. <i>Yac chreya etayor ekaṃ</i>, “which one of these two is better”. <i>Tan me brūhi suniścitam</i>, “tell me that with certainty”. Commentators note that Arjuna’s question echoes 3.2 almost exactly. Chapter 4 ended with knowledge burning karma (4.37) and with the call to take up yoga (4.42), so Arjuna is puzzled again. Śaṅkara uses this question to argue that the two cannot be practised together by one person. Rāmānuja says Arjuna wants to know which is easier. The question’s recurrence shows how hard this point is.",
 "If you have asked the same question twice, it is a real question. Keep asking until the answer fits.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Sannyāsaḥ karma-yogaś ca</i>, “renunciation and the yoga of action”. <i>Niḥśreyasa-karāv ubhau</i>, “both lead to the highest good”. <i>Tayos tu karma-sannyāsāt</i>, “but of the two, than renunciation of action”. <i>Karma-yogo viśiṣyate</i>, “karma yoga is superior”.",
 "Commentators wrestle with why Kṛṣṇa calls karma yoga better if both lead to the same goal. Śaṅkara: “better” means better for Arjuna and for most people, because outer renunciation without inner readiness fails; true knowledge-renunciation is for the few. Rāmānuja: karma yoga is better because it is easier, does not risk the dangers of a half-renunciation, and already contains self-knowledge. Madhva: karma yoga done as worship is better than a renunciation that may hide pride. Tilak took this verse as the Gītā’s clear preference for an active life.",
 "For most of us, the surer path is not to leave our work but to change how we do it.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Jñeyaḥ sa nitya-sannyāsī</i>, “he should be known as a constant renunciant”. <i>Yo na dveṣṭi na kāṅkṣati</i>, “who neither hates nor desires”. <i>Nirdvandvo hi mahā-bāho</i>, “free of opposites, mighty-armed one”. <i>Sukhaṃ bandhāt pramucyate</i>, “is easily freed from bondage”.",
 "<i>Nitya-sannyāsī</i>, “always a renunciant”, is a striking phrase for a karma yogi who still works. Commentators note the shift from outer to inner: renunciation is not about robes or leaving home, but about likes and dislikes. The same definition returns in 6.1: the true sannyāsī and yogi is one who does his duty without depending on its fruit, not one who merely gives up the sacred fire. Śaṅkara agrees that such a karma yogi is a renunciant in spirit. Rāmānuja says this inner renunciation is the essence of both paths. <i>Sukham</i>, “easily”, is encouraging.",
 "You can renounce without leaving home. Let go of one grudge or one craving today; that is real renunciation.")
DEEP[4]=("<i>Sāṅkhya-yogau pṛthag bālāḥ</i>, “children speak of Sāṅkhya and yoga as different”. <i>Pravadanti na paṇḍitāḥ</i>, “not the wise”. <i>Ekam apy āsthitaḥ samyag</i>, “one properly established even in one”. <i>Ubhayor vindate phalam</i>, “gains the fruit of both”.",
 "<i>Sāṅkhya</i> here means the path of knowledge and renunciation, as in 2.39, not the later philosophical school. <i>Bāla</i>, child, is a gentle word: not foolish, just not yet mature. Commentators see the Gītā’s typical reconciling spirit here. Śaṅkara says the karma yogi, through purity of mind, reaches the knowledge that the renunciant has; so the fruit is one. Rāmānuja says both lead to self-realisation. The verse has been used through history to calm quarrels between schools of practice. Ramakrishna’s teaching, “as many faiths, so many paths”, carries a similar spirit.",
 "Instead of arguing over which method is best, master one. Depth in one path brings what the others offer.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Yat sāṅkhyaiḥ prāpyate sthānaṃ</i>, “the state reached by the followers of knowledge”. <i>Tad yogair api gamyate</i>, “is reached by the yogis too”. <i>Ekaṃ sāṅkhyaṃ ca yogaṃ ca</i>, “knowledge and yoga as one”. <i>Yaḥ paśyati sa paśyati</i>, “who sees thus, truly sees”.",
 "The phrase <i>yaḥ paśyati sa paśyati</i>, “he who sees, sees”, appears again in 13.27. Commentators call it the Gītā’s mark of real vision. Śaṅkara says those who renounce reach liberation directly through knowledge; karma yogis reach it through knowledge that arises from purity of mind. The destination is one. Rāmānuja says both reach self-realisation, though karma yoga is safer. Madhva says both lead to God when done with devotion. The verse does not erase differences in method; it denies a difference in the final goal.",
 "Different people climb different paths. Seeing the shared summit makes room for respect.")
DEEP[6]=("<i>Sannyāsas tu mahā-bāho</i>, “but renunciation, mighty-armed one”. <i>Duḥkham āptum ayogataḥ</i>, “is hard to attain without yoga”. <i>Yoga-yukto munir brahma</i>, “the sage joined in yoga, to Brahman”. <i>Na cireṇādhigacchati</i>, “goes before long”.",
 "<i>Duḥkham</i> here means “with difficulty” or “painful”. Commentators explain: renunciation without prior purification of the mind is like a fruit plucked before it ripens. It is sour, and it falls. Śaṅkara says karma yoga is the preparation that makes true renunciation natural. Rāmānuja says karma yoga itself brings one to the vision of the self quickly. History has many examples of seekers who renounced too soon and struggled. Many teachers advise service and duty first; renunciation, if it comes, comes ripe.",
 "Before making a dramatic change in life, check whether the inner ground is ready. Ripeness matters.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Yoga-yukto viśuddhātmā</i>, “joined in yoga, pure of heart”. <i>Vijitātmā jitendriyaḥ</i>, “self-conquered, senses mastered”. <i>Sarva-bhūtātma-bhūtātmā</i>, “whose self has become the self of all beings”. <i>Kurvann api na lipyate</i>, “even acting, is not stained”.",
 "<i>Sarva-bhūtātma-bhūtātmā</i> is a long compound: one whose self has become the self of all beings. Commentators find here the ethical heart of the Gītā’s vision. Śaṅkara: such a person knows the one Self in all, so cannot act selfishly. Rāmānuja: such a person sees all selves as equal in nature to his own, and treats them so. The order of qualities is a ladder: yoga, purity, self-mastery, sense control, and then the universal vision. The phrase <i>na lipyate</i>, “not stained”, connects to the lotus image of 5.10.",
 "Treat others as you would treat yourself, not as a rule but as a way of seeing. Right action follows naturally.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Naiva kiñcit karomīti</i>, “‘I do nothing at all’”. <i>Yukto manyeta tattva-vit</i>, “the yogi who knows the truth thinks”. <i>Paśyañ śṛṇvan spṛśañ jighrann aśnan gacchan svapañ śvasan</i>, “seeing, hearing, touching, smelling, eating, walking, sleeping, breathing”.",
 "Verses 8–9 list thirteen activities: the five senses, eating, walking, sleeping, breathing, speaking, letting go, grasping and blinking. Commentators explain that the knower sees all of these as the work of the senses and organs, products of nature, while the Self remains a witness. Śaṅkara builds on this to describe the knower’s freedom from all agency. Rāmānuja says the self, knowing it is distinct from the body, does not take the body’s actions as its own. Teachers warn against using this as an excuse: the vision comes from knowledge, not from denial.",
 "Try watching one ordinary act, such as walking or eating, as if from a quiet place behind the eyes. Notice the calm.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Pralapan visṛjan gṛhṇann</i>, “speaking, letting go, grasping”. <i>Unmiṣan nimiṣann api</i>, “opening and closing the eyes”. <i>Indriyāṇīndriyārtheṣu vartanta iti dhārayan</i>, “holding that the senses move among their objects”.",
 "<i>Unmiṣan nimiṣan</i>, opening and closing the eyes, is the smallest of acts, chosen to show that the vision covers everything. Commentators link the phrase <i>indriyāṇīndriyārtheṣu vartante</i> to 3.28, <i>guṇā guṇeṣu vartante</i>: the senses move among their objects, nature among nature. Śaṅkara stresses that this knowledge is not a pretence but the real seeing of one who knows the Self. Madhusūdana compares it to watching a play: the actors move, but the audience is not on stage. The image helps meditators observe sensations without identifying with them.",
 "When a strong reaction rises, say quietly: “the senses are meeting their objects”. It creates a small, useful space.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Brahmaṇy ādhāya karmāṇi</i>, “placing actions in Brahman”. <i>Saṅgaṃ tyaktvā karoti yaḥ</i>, “who acts giving up attachment”. <i>Lipyate na sa pāpena</i>, “is not stained by sin”. <i>Padma-patram ivāmbhasā</i>, “like a lotus leaf by water”.",
 "The lotus leaf is waxy; water beads on it and rolls off. The image is old in Indian thought. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (4.14.3) says that as water does not cling to a lotus leaf, so evil does not cling to one who knows. Commentators of every school treasure the image. Śaṅkara reads “Brahman” as the Lord, to whom actions are offered. Rāmānuja reads it as prakṛti, nature, to whom actions belong, or as the Lord. The lotus is also a symbol of purity rising from mud, held by Viṣṇu and Lakṣmī. The image has passed into many languages as a model of living in the world without being stained by it.",
 "You don’t have to leave the pond. Grow your leaf so that what is not yours rolls off.")
DEEP[11]=("<i>Kāyena manasā buddhyā</i>, “with body, mind, intellect”. <i>Kevalair indriyair api</i>, “and with the senses alone”. <i>Yoginaḥ karma kurvanti</i>, “yogis act”. <i>Saṅgaṃ tyaktvātma-śuddhaye</i>, “giving up attachment, for purification of the self”.",
 "<i>Ātma-śuddhi</i>, purification of the self, here means of the mind and heart. Commentators explain the logic of karma yoga. Each action done without attachment loosens the grip of selfishness, and over time the mind becomes clear enough for knowledge. Śaṅkara calls this <i>citta-śuddhi</i>, purity of mind, the purpose of all action for the seeker. Rāmānuja says it removes the impressions of past karma. The verse names four instruments: body, mind, intellect and senses. All of them can be used for purification. Even the work of the hands is spiritual practice.",
 "Each task done without grasping is a small cleaning of the heart. Over a lifetime, it adds up.")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Yuktaḥ karma-phalaṃ tyaktvā</i>, “the one in yoga, giving up the fruit”. <i>Śāntim āpnoti naiṣṭhikīm</i>, “attains lasting peace”. <i>Ayuktaḥ kāma-kāreṇa</i>, “the one not in yoga, by the force of desire”. <i>Phale sakto nibadhyate</i>, “attached to the fruit, is bound”.",
 "<i>Naiṣṭhikī śānti</i>, a peace that is steady and settled, contrasts with the temporary relief of getting what we want. Commentators note that the outer work of the yukta and the ayukta can look identical. The farmer, the doctor and the soldier do the same things either way. The difference is inner: attachment to fruit binds; giving it up brings peace. Śaṅkara says the peace is that of liberation. Rāmānuja says it is peace in the self. The verse is often quoted for its simple psychology: attachment produces anxiety; release produces calm.",
 "If your work is making you anxious, check how tightly you are holding the result.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Sarva-karmāṇi manasā sannyasya</i>, “renouncing all actions with the mind”. <i>Āste sukhaṃ vaśī</i>, “the self-controlled one rests happily”. <i>Nava-dvāre pure dehī</i>, “the embodied one, in the city of nine gates”. <i>Naiva kurvan na kārayan</i>, “neither doing nor causing to be done”.",
 "The “city of nine gates” image is found in the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (2.2.1), which speaks of a city of eleven gates, and the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (3.18), which speaks of nine. Commentators read the body as a city and the self as its king or resident. Śaṅkara says the knower dwells in the body without identifying with it, like a traveller in an inn. Rāmānuja says the self rests in the body, knowing it is distinct. The phrase <i>manasā</i>, “with the mind”, again stresses that the renunciation is inner. The image invites a calm, almost royal relationship with one’s own body.",
 "Treat your body like a well-kept house you live in: care for it, but don’t mistake it for yourself.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Na kartṛtvaṃ na karmāṇi</i>, “neither doership nor actions”. <i>Lokasya sṛjati prabhuḥ</i>, “does the Lord create for the world”. <i>Na karma-phala-saṃyogaṃ</i>, “nor the link of action and fruit”. <i>Svabhāvas tu pravartate</i>, “but nature works”.",
 "This verse is subtle and much discussed. <i>Prabhu</i>, the Lord or master, is read by Śaṅkara as the Self: the Self does not act or cause action; ignorance, called nature, does. Rāmānuja reads <i>prabhu</i> as the individual self, the master of the body. The self does not, of its own nature, create doership; that comes from its contact with matter. Madhva reads <i>prabhu</i> as God, who governs but leaves individuals responsible for their nature. All agree on the practical point: our own nature and habits drive our actions, not some outside force.",
 "If you want to change what you do, look at your habits. That is where the steering wheel is.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Nādatte kasyacit pāpaṃ</i>, “takes no one’s sin”. <i>Na caiva sukṛtaṃ vibhuḥ</i>, “nor merit, the all-pervading one”. <i>Ajñānenāvṛtaṃ jñānaṃ</i>, “knowledge is covered by ignorance”. <i>Tena muhyanti jantavaḥ</i>, “by that, creatures are deluded”.",
 "<i>Vibhu</i>, the all-pervading, is the Self or the Lord. Commentators say this verse names the root problem of the whole Gītā: not sin, but ignorance covering knowledge. Śaṅkara: the Self is never touched by merit or sin; ignorance makes us think it is. Rāmānuja: the Lord does not take on anyone’s karma; beings are deluded by their past karma, which hides the self’s true nature. The image of covering recalls 3.38, fire covered by smoke. The good news is implied: what is covered can be uncovered.",
 "Much of our struggle is not lack of goodness but lack of seeing. Clearing the view can change everything.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Jñānena tu tad ajñānaṃ</i>, “but by knowledge, that ignorance”. <i>Yeṣāṃ nāśitam ātmanaḥ</i>, “for those in whom it is destroyed, of the Self”. <i>Teṣām ādityavaj jñānaṃ</i>, “for them, like the sun, knowledge”. <i>Prakāśayati tat param</i>, “reveals that Supreme”.",
 "<i>Ādityavat</i>, “like the sun”. Commentators explain the image: the sun does not create objects; it shows what is already there. Likewise knowledge does not create the Self; it removes ignorance and reveals it. Śaṅkara says the Self is self-luminous; knowledge simply removes the cloud. Rāmānuja says the self’s knowledge, once freed from karma, shines like the sun and shows its true nature. The Gāyatrī mantra also prays to the light of the sun to illumine the mind; many teachers link the two.",
 "Understanding does not add something new; it shows what was there. Look for clarity rather than novelty.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Tad-buddhayas tad-ātmānas</i>, “whose understanding is in That, whose self is That”. <i>Tan-niṣṭhās tat-parāyaṇāḥ</i>, “established in That, with That as the goal”. <i>Gacchanty apunar-āvṛttiṃ</i>, “go to the state of no return”. <i>Jñāna-nirdhūta-kalmaṣāḥ</i>, “their sins shaken off by knowledge”.",
 "The word <i>tat</i>, “That”, is used four times: understanding, self, foundation, goal. It recalls the great Upaniṣadic saying <i>tat tvam asi</i>, “you are That” (Chāndogya 6.8.7). Commentators say the fourfold repetition shows total dedication: what one thinks, who one is, where one stands, where one is going. Śaṅkara reads “That” as Brahman, one with the Self. Rāmānuja reads it as the self, or the Lord. <i>Apunar-āvṛtti</i>, no return, means freedom from rebirth, described again in 8.21 and 15.6.",
 "When thought, identity and goal point the same way, life gains great power and peace.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Vidyā-vinaya-sampanne</i>, “endowed with learning and humility”. <i>Brāhmaṇe gavi hastini</i>, “in a Brahmin, a cow, an elephant”. <i>Śuni caiva śvapāke ca</i>, “in a dog and an outcaste”. <i>Paṇḍitāḥ sama-darśinaḥ</i>, “the wise see equally”.",
 "This verse is one of the Gītā’s boldest. Commentators note the deliberate range: the most honoured person (a learned, humble Brahmin), a sacred animal (the cow), the largest animal (the elephant), an animal thought impure (the dog), and the person society placed lowest (the <i>śvapāka</i>). The wise see the same in all. Śaṅkara says the wise see the one Brahman in all, untouched by differences of form. Rāmānuja says the wise see the selves in all as equal in nature, all made of knowledge. A famous story tells that Śaṅkara himself was challenged at Kāśī by an outcaste and recognised the teacher in him, composing the Manīṣā Pañcakam. Many modern reformers, including Gandhi and Narayana Guru, drew on this verse.",
 "Before judging someone by status or appearance, remember this verse. The same light looks out from every face.")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Ihaiva tair jitaḥ sargo</i>, “even here, the world is conquered by them”. <i>Yeṣāṃ sāmye sthitaṃ manaḥ</i>, “whose minds are established in evenness”. <i>Nirdoṣaṃ hi samaṃ brahma</i>, “Brahman is flawless and even”. <i>Tasmād brahmaṇi te sthitāḥ</i>, “therefore they are established in Brahman”.",
 "<i>Ihaiva</i>, “even here”, is key: freedom is possible in this life. Commentators call this state <i>jīvanmukti</i>, liberation while living. <i>Sarga</i>, the world of birth and becoming, is “conquered” in the sense that it no longer binds. Śaṅkara says the even mind sees the one Brahman everywhere, and that seeing is itself freedom. Rāmānuja says those whose minds see all selves as equal have conquered rebirth. Brahman is called <i>sama</i>, even, because it is the same in all; to be even in mind is to share Brahman’s nature.",
 "Evenness is not a distant goal. Each moment of steady fairness is a taste of it now.")
DEEP[20]=("<i>Na prahṛṣyet priyaṃ prāpya</i>, “let him not be elated on getting the pleasant”. <i>Nodvijet prāpya cāpriyam</i>, “nor agitated on getting the unpleasant”. <i>Sthira-buddhir asammūḍho</i>, “steady in understanding, undeluded”. <i>Brahma-vid brahmaṇi sthitaḥ</i>, “the knower of Brahman, established in Brahman”.",
 "This verse repeats themes from 2.56–57 and 2.70, now applied to the knower of Brahman. Commentators note the gentle verbs: <i>prahṛṣ</i>, to be thrilled, and <i>udvij</i>, to be shaken or alarmed. The sage still feels, but is not thrown. Śaṅkara says steadiness comes from knowing that both pleasant and unpleasant belong to the body, not the Self. Rāmānuja says the knower of the self sees both as passing results of past karma. Modern readers often find this verse a practical guide for emotional resilience.",
 "Next time good news or bad news comes, take one breath before reacting. That breath is where steadiness grows.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Bāhya-sparśeṣv asaktātmā</i>, “unattached to outer contacts”. <i>Vindaty ātmani yat sukham</i>, “finds the joy that is in the Self”. <i>Sa brahma-yoga-yuktātmā</i>, “joined by yoga to Brahman”. <i>Sukham akṣayam aśnute</i>, “enjoys imperishable happiness”.",
 "<i>Akṣaya sukha</i>, “undecaying happiness”, contrasts with the pleasures born of contact (5.22), which begin and end. Commentators explain that inner joy is not created; it is found, <i>vindati</i>, because it was always there. Śaṅkara identifies it with the bliss of Brahman. Rāmānuja identifies it with the joy of the self’s own nature, and ultimately of the Lord. The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (2.8) describes a ladder of joys, each a hundred times greater than the last, ending in the joy of Brahman. The Gītā points to the top of that ladder.",
 "Look for moments of joy that need nothing outside. Return to them often; they grow with attention.")
DEEP[22]=("<i>Ye hi saṃsparśajā bhogā</i>, “the pleasures born of contact”. <i>Duḥkha-yonaya eva te</i>, “are wombs of sorrow”. <i>Ādy-antavantaḥ kaunteya</i>, “they have beginning and end, son of Kuntī”. <i>Na teṣu ramate budhaḥ</i>, “the wise do not delight in them”.",
 "Commentators explain why pleasures are “wombs of sorrow”. They cause pain when they end, they create craving for more, and getting them often involves conflict or harm. The Yoga Sūtra (2.15) makes the same point: to the discerning, all is sorrow, because of change, anxiety, impressions and the conflict of the guṇas. The Buddha’s first noble truth, <i>dukkha</i>, is related. The Gītā does not ask us to hate pleasure but not to build our happiness on it. Śaṅkara says the wise see through pleasure; Rāmānuja says they see it as tiny compared to the joy of the self.",
 "Enjoy pleasant things, but don’t lean your whole weight on them. They are visitors, not foundations.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Śaknotīhaiva yaḥ soḍhuṃ</i>, “who can bear here”. <i>Prāk śarīra-vimokṣaṇāt</i>, “before release from the body”. <i>Kāma-krodhodbhavaṃ vegaṃ</i>, “the surge born of desire and anger”. <i>Sa yuktaḥ sa sukhī naraḥ</i>, “he is a yogi, he is happy”.",
 "<i>Vega</i> means a surge, rush or impulse, like a river in flood. Commentators note the realism: desire and anger will come; the yogi is one who can bear them without being swept away. <i>Prāk śarīra-vimokṣaṇāt</i>, “before leaving the body”, adds urgency: this is the work of this life. Śaṅkara says even at the moment of death, one who can withstand these is a yogi. Rāmānuja says the practice must be done now, while we can. Modern psychology speaks of “urge surfing”, letting an impulse rise and fall without acting on it, a technique very close to this verse.",
 "Next time an angry or craving wave rises, try just watching it rise and fall. You may be surprised how fast it passes.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Yo’ntaḥ-sukho’ntar-ārāmas</i>, “whose happiness is within, whose delight is within”. <i>Tathāntar-jyotir eva yaḥ</i>, “and whose light is within”. <i>Sa yogī brahma-nirvāṇaṃ</i>, “that yogi, to the peace of Brahman”. <i>Brahma-bhūto’dhigacchati</i>, “having become Brahman, goes”.",
 "Three “withins”: happiness, delight and light. Commentators note the progression: <i>sukha</i>, a quiet joy; <i>ārāma</i>, a garden of delight; <i>jyoti</i>, inner light, knowledge. <i>Brahma-bhūta</i>, having become Brahman, appears again in 18.54. Śaṅkara reads it as complete identity with Brahman. Rāmānuja reads it as the self attaining a nature like Brahman’s, pure and free. <i>Brahma-nirvāṇa</i> recalls 2.72 and returns in the next two verses. The three “withins” make a fine meditation: rest in inner joy, inner delight, inner light.",
 "Spend a few minutes today looking inward for something that is already joyful and bright. It is there.")
DEEP[25]=("<i>Labhante brahma-nirvāṇam</i>, “they attain the peace of Brahman”. <i>Ṛṣayaḥ kṣīṇa-kalmaṣāḥ</i>, “the seers whose sins are destroyed”. <i>Chinna-dvaidhā yatātmānaḥ</i>, “whose doubts are cut, who are self-controlled”. <i>Sarva-bhūta-hite ratāḥ</i>, “delighting in the welfare of all beings”.",
 "<i>Sarva-bhūta-hite ratāḥ</i>, “rejoicing in the good of all beings”, returns in 12.4. Commentators note that the Gītā’s sage is not a cold hermit but a person of active goodwill. Śaṅkara says such sages avoid harming any being. Rāmānuja says they delight in the welfare of others because they see all as like themselves. The four marks form a path: purification (sins destroyed), clarity (doubts cut), self-control, and universal goodwill. Many modern teachers, including Vivekananda, emphasised this verse to link spirituality with service.",
 "Find joy in others’ good. It is one of the surest signs, and causes, of inner peace.")
DEEP[26]=("<i>Kāma-krodha-viyuktānāṃ</i>, “of those freed from desire and anger”. <i>Yatīnāṃ yata-cetasām</i>, “of ascetics with controlled minds”. <i>Abhito brahma-nirvāṇaṃ</i>, “near on all sides is the peace of Brahman”. <i>Vartate viditātmanām</i>, “for those who know the Self”.",
 "<i>Abhitaḥ</i>, “on all sides” or “near”, is read by commentators as both here and hereafter: such people live in peace and die in peace. Śaṅkara says liberation surrounds them in life and after death. Rāmānuja says the vision of the self is near for them. The verse repeats the freedom from desire and anger that runs through the Gītā (2.56, 3.37, 4.10, 5.23, 5.28, 16.21). It also sets the stage for the meditation preview of 5.27–28 and the full teaching on meditation in chapter 6.",
 "Peace is closer than it seems. Quiet the inner noise of wanting and resentment, and it surrounds you.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Sparśān kṛtvā bahir bāhyān</i>, “making outer contacts outside”. <i>Cakṣuś caivāntare bhruvoḥ</i>, “and the gaze between the eyebrows”. <i>Prāṇāpānau samau kṛtvā</i>, “making in-breath and out-breath even”. <i>Nāsābhyantara-cāriṇau</i>, “moving within the nostrils”.",
 "Verses 5.27–28 preview the meditation of chapter 6 in miniature. Commentators note three steps. First, <i>pratyāhāra</i>, withdrawing the senses from outer contacts. Second, <i>dhāraṇā</i>, fixing attention, here at the point between the eyebrows, a classic focus in yoga traditions. Third, <i>prāṇāyāma</i>, evening the breath. Śaṅkara explains that the gaze is held there to prevent the eyes from wandering or falling asleep. Rāmānuja reads it as the posture of meditation on the self. The same focus point appears in 8.10, where the dying yogi fixes the breath between the eyebrows.",
 "Sit for one minute: eyes softly closed, attention between the brows, breath slow and even. That is a real meditation.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Yatendriya-mano-buddhir</i>, “with senses, mind and intellect controlled”. <i>Munir mokṣa-parāyaṇaḥ</i>, “the sage intent on freedom”. <i>Vigatecchā-bhaya-krodho</i>, “free of desire, fear and anger”. <i>Yaḥ sadā mukta eva saḥ</i>, “is ever free”.",
 "<i>Sadā mukta eva</i>, “ever free indeed”: commentators note the strong statement. Freedom is not a future prize but the present state of such a person. Śaṅkara reads this as liberation while living. Rāmānuja says such a person is as good as liberated. The triad <i>icchā, bhaya, krodha</i>, desire, fear and anger, appears once again. The order is the ladder of chapter 3 in reverse: senses, mind, intellect controlled; desire, fear and anger gone; freedom here. The full method follows in chapter 6.",
 "Each time desire, fear or anger fades, notice the lightness. That lightness is freedom, already tasted.")
DEEP[29]=("<i>Bhoktāraṃ yajña-tapasāṃ</i>, “the enjoyer of sacrifices and austerities”. <i>Sarva-loka-maheśvaram</i>, “the great Lord of all worlds”. <i>Suhṛdaṃ sarva-bhūtānāṃ</i>, “the friend of all beings”. <i>Jñātvā māṃ śāntim ṛcchati</i>, “knowing me, one attains peace”.",
 "Three titles of the Lord: enjoyer of all offerings, Lord of all worlds, friend of all beings. Commentators find the third the most moving. <i>Suhṛt</i> means a good-hearted friend, one who helps without expecting return. Rāmānuja says knowing the Lord as such a friend brings complete trust, and trust brings peace. Śaṅkara says the Lord is the inner Self of all, the source of all good. Madhva stresses the Lord’s sovereignty and kindness together. Chapter 5 ends not with discipline but with relationship. The colophon names it Karma-Sannyāsa Yoga.",
 "The power behind the universe is, in this view, a friend. Let that thought soften whatever you are facing today.")
